in southwestern China engineers are  putting the finishing touches on a grand  astronomy facility a half kilometre wide  dish nestled in the mountains that will  gather radio signals from the cosmos  fast set to be the world's largest radio  telescope is modeled on the second  largest dish in the world the Arecibo  Observatory started in 2011 the cost of  construction for the fast facility is  estimated to be a hundred and eighty  million dollars spherical dishes reflect  and concentrate incoming waves from a  range of angles but not on to a single  point delivering a distorted image to  receivers  fast is breaking new ground with a  design that pulls a section of the dish  into a gradually moving parabola an  array of actuators pull a 300 meter  section into a temporary shape this can  be aimed at and tracked cosmic objects  as the Earth rotates the receiver moves  in concert with the axis of the parabola  this brings the benefits of a rotating  tilting antenna to a dish that is firmly  fixed in place single dishes excel at  observing point sources like neutron  stars and it's scanning a multitude of  frequencies in this search for  extraterrestrial intelligence when it  comes online the world's largest radio  telescope will also catalog pulsars as  well as probe gravitational waves dark  matter and a recently discovered  phenomenon called fast radio bursts  you
 

